    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nGM Defense Presents Rugged Chevrolet Silverado 3500 HD With No Driver Looking Like a 'Skateboard'\n10 photos\nPhoto: GM Defense\nGeneral Motors , the largest US automaker based in Detroit, likes to dip its toes into just about everything, including military stuff, and just recently its GM Defense subsidiary came to the Association of the United States Army (AUSA) Annual Meeting and Exposition to showcase something new and intriguing.\nGeneral Motors may not be a part of the roster for the 2026 Paris Motor Show going on these days (October 12-18, 2026) at the Paris Expo Porte de Versailles in France, but it’s no stranger to big events. At least its GM Defense subsidiary, which went to the Association of the United States Army (AUSA) Annual Meeting and Exposition to present the “rapid acceleration of its global business model.”\nThe company says it’s guided (like a homing missile) by a “customer-first, technology-transferred” approach, as it aims to bridge the gap “between commercial innovation and tactical military deployment at unprecedented speeds.” The U.S. Army’s planned requirements have ballooned GM Defense’s Infantry Squad Vehicle order from the initial batch of 649 vehicles to no less than 12k units, mostly because of the success of their Infantry Squad Vehicle Utility (ISV-U) prototype.\nThe latter will be turned into a highly versatile five-soldier variant that will be fielded across 39 active-duty and National Guard mobile brigades, while its contract with Lockheed Martin for the mission-critical PAC-3 Missile Segment Enhancement (MSE) housing components was honored just 22 days after the signing of the contract in August.\nMore importantly, at the event, GM Defense revealed its brand-new Unmanned Ground Vehicle (UGV) concept, created on the basis of a rugged Chevrolet Silverado 3500 HD dual-rear-wheel model now fitted with a skateboard architecture to support silent logistics and casualty evacuation. First functional trials are coming in 2027, and GM also showcased the EPIQ Hybrid prototype, a customer-inspired 60-kilowatt/100-kilowatt-hour expeditionary energy storage system paired with an onboard generator.\nIt was created to “lower thermal and noise signatures in austere environments,” while subsidiaries like GM Defence UK will advance their own programs – the latter company has the Light Mobility Vehicle program proposal made together with Team LionStrike with NP Aerospace and BAE Systems, focusing on a low-risk, high-quality, commercially based solution operating at the speed and scale required by the allied forces.\n“The modern defense landscape demands advanced capability, rapid fielding and sustainable scale,” said Steve duMont, president of GM Defense. “We do not begin with a blank sheet of paper. By transferring GM’s world-class engineering, supply chain, and manufacturing scale directly to the service member, we are transforming customer needs into mission-ready capability faster than ever before.”\nAs a reminder, GM Defense focuses on \"integrated vehicles; power and propulsion; and autonomy and connectivity solutions to global defense, security and government markets.\" It proposes OEM quality and world-class manufacturing scale for rapid deployment of contracts.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGeneral Motors GM chevrolet silverado 3500 hd Chevrolet Silvrado 3500 HD autonomous truck gm defense\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Kia's New EV2 Crossover Just Took Five Euro NCAP Stars",
